# builder agent rules

Mirror of `Rules.md`. Prefer on-disk `Rules.md` if they diverge.

## General

- Responses must be concise and deliver only the information requested. If you have something other than a direct answer to a question to say, confirm that the user wants to hear it before wasting tokens.
- Comments should be concise and describe intent rather than behaviour.
- The user edits code outside Cursor — always work from the most recently saved on-disk file.
- Never edit from chat history or memory: before every change, re-read that file from disk in the same turn; base the edit only on what you just read; do not rewrite a whole file from an earlier version in the conversation.
- Never use PowerShell. On this machine the Cursor Shell tool always wraps commands in PowerShell, so do not use the Shell tool at all — not even with `cmd /c`. Use Read / Write / StrReplace / Grep / Glob / Delete instead. If a command must be run (pyright, app launch, build), ask the user to run it and paste the output.
- No mutable module-level globals; pass state explicitly (immutable constants are fine).
- Do not obfuscate ownership with forwarding properties or thin wrappers that re-expose nested state under a flatter name (eg. `selected_ids` that only returns `self.nodes.selected_ids`). Access the owning object directly so it is obvious what the data belongs to.
- No redundant indirection: do not invent aliases, parallel catalogs, or normalize helpers that only restate something already usable (eg. building `axis_choices` from `Axis`, or `param_options` that unpacks an enum the caller could use). Use the source value at the call site; if the API cannot accept it, change the API.
- No magic-name / sniffing designs: do not wire behaviour by `hasattr`, string field names, or silent conventions that guess which data applies (eg. `if role == "point" and hasattr(params, "point_orient")`). The link between data and behaviour must be explicit and typed at the call site — if the shape is wrong, fix the types, do not invent a helper that papers over it.

## Style

- Do not use the `_method` convention; just name the methods.
- Do not name modules `types.py`; use `modulename_types.py`.
- **US spelling** in prose, comments, and identifiers (not UK spelling).
- Docstrings and comments: lowercase, no trailing period — eg. `"""return transforms for grid in XZ centered at origin"""`.

## Imports / types / design

- Do not shorten library names (e.g. `pyray`, not `pr`).
- Prefer individual imports over importing full modules.
- For pyray enum-like constants, import the enum type and use members (eg. `ConfigFlags.FLAG_WINDOW_RESIZABLE`) — bare names may work at runtime but fail the type checker.
- Prefer a functional style with dataclasses and pure functions where possible; classes only with discussion.
- Strict type annotations on every variable at first declaration.

## Architecture / data model

- Do not force a feature into the wrong abstraction. Generators are instance-placement recipes (transforms → meshed children of shared catalog meshes). Continuous surfaces / terrains are first-class node recipes (eg. `Node.heightfield`), not another generator kind.
- Recipe fields that drive UI/build use typed param wrappers (`IntParam`, `FloatParam`, …); do not revive ParamField maps or string-field sniffing for recipes.
- Prefer IntEnum over string/`Literal` discriminators; serialize enums by **name** in scene JSON, decode name-only — no int fallbacks.
- No backwards compatibility in scene load unless explicitly requested: no migrators, legacy aliases, or soft version paths. Bump `scene_format_version` when the format changes; reject mismatches.
- Regenerated children (generator slots, heightfield tiles) are omitted on save and rebuilt on load; keep mesh/tile ids deterministic from parent id + indices for later LOD/streaming.
- Separate catalogs for different asset kinds (meshes vs heightmaps); do not overload `MeshCatalog` for non-mesh assets.
- Design for large scale early when asked (tiling, sample-per-tile, no full-resolution buffers) but ship the smallest working slice the user picks; leave hooks, do not build LOD/streaming until asked.
- Keep package `__init__` slim; domain code must not import `command_context` / command modules — use a local Protocol for the host surface needed. Match exhaustively when adding enum members; mirror existing UI action enums (`TextAction.editing`, not invented variants).
